# Speckit Specification

## Overview
This document defines the intended behavior, requirements, and acceptance criteria for the project. It is intended to guide development, testing, and validation.

## Scope
- Define user-facing features and flows.
- Capture non-functional requirements for quality, performance, and consistency.
- Provide clear acceptance criteria for implementation and review.

## Requirements

### Functional Requirements
- Users can access and use the main application features with predictable behavior.
- The system should clearly surface state, actions, and outcomes.
- Inputs must be validated and errors should be communicated in a user-friendly way.
- Shared components should behave consistently across pages and interactions.

### Non-Functional Requirements
- Code should be maintainable, modular, and easy to understand.
- Automated tests must exist for core functionality and regressions.
- The user interface must be consistent and accessible.
- Performance should remain responsive across standard devices and networks.

## Acceptance Criteria
- All user flows are documented and have defined success and failure conditions.
- Each feature has associated test cases and a clear pass/fail definition.
- UI states are consistent, and any deviations are approved through design review.
- Performance goals are measured and validated before release.

## Specification Format
For each feature or requirement, include:
- Title
- Description
- User story
- Success criteria
- Edge cases
- Test cases

## Example
### Feature: User Onboarding
- Description: Provide a first-time setup flow for new users.
- User story: As a new user, I want to complete the onboarding steps so I can begin using the application.
- Success criteria:
  - The onboarding flow loads successfully.
  - Inputs are validated and errors are shown.
  - Completion saves the correct user preferences.
- Edge cases:
  - Submissions with missing data.
  - Slow network responses.
- Test cases:
  - Validate successful flow completion.
  - Verify error messages appear for invalid input.

## Implementation Notes
- Keep specifications concise and actionable.
- Use this document as a reference during development and review.
- Update it whenever requirements change or new scenarios are added.
